= Proposed AERO Course Descriptions
Working draft · September 28, 2026

== Advanced Drone Mapping

This course prepares students to plan and complete drone mapping projects from initial site assessment through delivery of accurate spatial data. Students learn to select camera settings, design flight missions, and collect imagery suitable for photogrammetric processing. Field activities develop skills in high-accuracy positioning, ground control placement, and the use of independent checkpoints to evaluate map accuracy. Students process imagery into orthomosaics, point clouds, and elevation models while learning to recognize errors and document the limitations of their results. A final mapping project integrates mission planning, field operations, data processing, and professional reporting for applications in construction, agriculture, environmental monitoring, and public agencies.

== UAV Applications in Precision Agriculture

This course introduces the use of drones to monitor crop health, assess irrigation issues, and apply pesticides in agricultural settings. Students learn to collect and interpret visible, multispectral, and thermal imagery to identify crop variability, signs of stress, and areas requiring field investigation. Instruction connects aerial observations with crop scouting, irrigation assessment, and the development of maps that support targeted management decisions. Students also learn the principles and procedures of drone pesticide application, including mission planning, equipment calibration, application rates, and drift management. Throughout the course, emphasis is placed on safe operations, environmental protection, and the FAA and California requirements that govern agricultural drone use.

== FAA Regulations for Drone Pilots

This one-unit, lecture-only course examines the FAA rules and regulatory processes that shape professional drone operations beyond introductory Part 107 preparation. Students learn to locate and interpret regulations, advisory guidance, and other FAA resources when evaluating proposed missions. Topics include operating limitations, airspace authorizations, aircraft registration, Remote Identification, and pilot responsibilities. Students examine the waiver application process and the operational safeguards and supporting documentation used to demonstrate that a proposed operation can be conducted safely. Case studies and discussion address emerging rules, including Part 108 rulemaking for operations beyond visual line of sight, and develop students' ability to distinguish proposed changes from requirements currently in effect.

== AERO Independent Study

This course provides an opportunity for individual study or practical experience in a selected aerospace topic under the direction of an instructor. Students develop an approved study plan that identifies learning objectives, activities, and methods of evaluation. Projects may involve research, technical investigation, or an applied aerospace or drone project that builds on prior coursework. Students meet with the instructor to review progress, address challenges, and refine their work. Completion requires a final report, presentation, or other agreed-upon product that demonstrates achievement of the study objectives.
